Data Engineer Report:

There were two different approaches that we used to help improve the baseline. The first approach was based on the idea of focusing on only analyzing the words in the chorus. In an ideal world we would identify the chorus by repeating phrases of words. However, our data is restricted because we don’t have the order of the words in the songs, just the count. So we can not look at what words are repeated next to each other. As a proxy, I focused on the top-k signature words in a song. The k that was chosen was 8. So I only looked at the 8 most common words.Ultimately, the result was a 15.5 percent accuracy. This was directly in between the raw inner product’s performance and the TF-IDF cosine similarity. So it was successful but not as efficient as the existing methods.

Another idea I was interested in looking at was the actual amount of repeated words by genre. Some genres would have more of a chorus and therefore repeat words more than other genres. If this is the case it may be worth including. What I looked at was what was the frequency of those signature words in the entire song. Different genres had very different rates of signature words. Latin was the highest frequency with 57 percent of the words being signature words and hip hop was the lowest with 36 percent of the words being signature words. However, when this was added to the top-k signature model the improvement was so minimal that it is not worth the additional noise in the data. 

In order to improve from the baselines, a helpful metric that I would want to look at is the pace of the song in words / minute. This would be very easy to implement if we had data on the runtime of the song. I think this would drastically improve the model because genres like hip/hop will have very high words / minutes and then more instrumental genres like classical will have very low words per minute.  
